    "message": "fix(analytics): the card said \"Total requests\" but counted stored rows\n\nReported twice as \"analytics resets after 50,070 requests\". Nothing\nresets: retention caps stored rows at REQUEST_LOG_MAX_ROWS and prune runs\nevery 100 inserts, so the count climbs to ~50,099 and drops back to\n50,000, over and over. A card labelled \"Total requests\" made that read as\na broken counter.\n\nIt now reads \"Stored requests\", with \"at the storage cap -- older ones\ndeleted\" underneath once the cap is reached. The label was the bug: the\nnumber was always right for what it counted.\n\nThe retention note now leads with the consequence that actually matters\nand was never stated: pruned requests are deleted, so they cannot be\nlisted, opened or searched. All-time counters keep counting, but they are\ncounts -- they do not bring rows back. Raising the cap is the only way to\nbrowse further back, and it quotes the new per-request cost (~7 KB\ncompressed against 41 KB before) so the disk trade is concrete.\n\nPage size gains 250 and 500 to match the limit the API already allows.\nTests pin that paging reaches every stored row and that the largest page\nsize the UI offers is accepted.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
